2026-09-28: FPU nested-result accounting RED -> GREEN, not full kernel acceptance.

Actual original fpu:test_fpu on the new GCC Image prints a missing-debugfs
[SKIP] and exits zero. The upstream runner emits an outer TAP PASS. The old
parser discarded the inner skip and could allow pair/campaign acceptance.
The original run_test_fpu.sh launches 1000 * online-CPU workers; its final
shell status also cannot establish every background worker's result.

The small C fixture preserves the worker's three source-bound protocols:
skip -> [SKIP], exit 0; fail -> [FAIL], exit 3; ok -> [OK], exit 0.
GCC14 and Clang19 compile with -std=c11 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror and execute
all six controls successfully in the sealed linux/arm64 image. No EvoCC
userspace code or altered upstream source is credited by this harness test.

Before the repair, three focused tests have five failed assertions: missing,
malformed, skipped and failing worker results are silently accepted/omitted.
The shared parser now retains scoped FPU worker PASS/FAIL/SKIP counts apart
from the outer TAP case, and both paired and full-campaign acceptance keep
nested failures/skips INCOMPLETE. A stress success also requires the original
iteration announcement and an exact 1000 * online-CPU message denominator.
The common init emits that CPU count bound to the nonce and selected program.
Missing/duplicate/foreign/zero/invalid count markers fail closed; no CPU count
is invented for historical logs. Counts survive full program_case mapping.
This verifies the frozen worker-message protocol, not a new adversarial log
authentication mechanism or uniquely identified per-PID event stream.

Six focused parser tests, the full-mapping test, all 282 K1 Python tests,
317 ladder tests, Ruff and diff whitespace checks pass. Missing/extra workers,
one changed failure, malformed/truncated statuses, unrelated output and
combined standalone/stress collection scopes have explicit controls.
GCC14 static init build and Clang19 object build pass. The first Clang build
reported that fatal/poweroff lacked non-returning declarations; the code's
existing infinite shutdown path now has truthful _Noreturn annotations rather
than fabricated initialization. That failed build log remains preserved.

Original unchanged reference replays with the new small init:
  fpu:test_fpu       outer PASS; 0 OK / 0 FAIL / 1 SKIP -> INCOMPLETE acceptance
  fpu:run_test_fpu.sh outer PASS; 4000/4000 OK, 0 FAIL, 0 SKIP; normal shutdown
No source, argument, module body, disk or 45-second upstream limit changes.
Only the tiny init payload changes; its old binary/cpio/build receipt remain.
The standalone debugfs prerequisite still needs restoration. EvoCC's new full
composition is still building; no matching subject-runtime result exists yet.

